# Report: attributing LLM text to its generator (RAID)

## Setup

The task is to identify which of five chat models (chatgpt, cohere-chat, llama-chat, mistral-chat or mpt-chat) wrote a text from the RAID benchmark. The data has 8 text domains with 400 source ids each. Ids were split 70/15/15 into train (2,240 ids), validation (480) and test (480), with 5 rows per id, so chance is 20%. Validation scores were used for model selection and are not a generalization estimate, so every figure below is a test score unless stated otherwise. All results come from a single split, except for TF-IDF, which also has grouped 5-fold cross-validation. Neural "ensemble" figures average the probabilities of several seeds and run higher than a single model, while TF-IDF is one deterministic fit. McNemar p-values are slightly optimistic because the five rows of a source id are not independent. The full tables are in `reports/results.md`.

## RQ1. Can we identify which LLM generated a response?

Yes, well above chance. Using only the output text, the CNN and BiLSTM reach about 66% to 73% single-seed accuracy, but a TF-IDF plus logistic regression baseline does as well or better. With the specified configuration and a 256-token output cap, the CNN scores 68.2% and the BiLSTM 65.7% (71.8% and 69.4% as seed ensembles). After tuning on validation data only and raising the cap (2,048 tokens for the CNN, 512 for the BiLSTM), they reach 72.7% and 70.5% (ensembles 76.3% and 73.8%). That gain of 3 to 4 points is significant after Holm correction. TF-IDF scores 75.8% at the 256-token cap and 78.7% without truncation (95% interval 77.0% to 80.3%), and its grouped cross-validation accuracy of 76.2% (plus or minus 0.6) matches its test result. The neural models were not cross-validated, so their only held-out number is the single test split. The tuned CNN ties TF-IDF at the same 256-token cap (p = 0.56) but trails full-text TF-IDF (p = 0.023), and the BiLSTM trails it clearly (p < 1e-8). Selecting the best of a few seeds is also optimistic: one 3-seed CNN result fell from 73.1 to 72.1 macro-F1 after two more seeds were added.

Most errors confuse mistral-chat with mpt-chat (121 of 582 TF-IDF errors). Recall is 79% to 88% for chatgpt, cohere-chat and llama-chat, against 61% for mistral-chat and 66% for mpt-chat. Accuracy also varies by domain, from 89.3% on reviews and 88.0% on recipes to 71.4% on reddit, 68.6% on wiki and 62.4% on news.

## RQ2. Does the user's prompt help identify the LLM?

A prompt alone does not help. TF-IDF and the majority baseline both score exactly 20.00%, which is chance. The reason is the data design: for every source id (3,200 of 3,200 text ids and 400 of 400 code ids), the prompt is identical across all five models, so it says nothing about which model answered. This is a property of the dataset and not a modelling failure. In a dataset where models received different prompts, a high prompt-only score would point to leakage and not to a real model fingerprint.

Adding the prompt to the output also gives no reliable gain. TF-IDF moves from 75.75% to 76.71% (Holm p = 0.16), the CNN ensemble from 71.83% to 71.29% (p = 0.94), and the BiLSTM ensemble from 69.42% to 70.58% (p = 0.36). None of these differences is significant. The tuned 5-seed ensembles show the same pattern, with point estimates within about 0.6 points for the CNN and 0.2 for the BiLSTM, although these pairs were not tested separately and the BiLSTM runs with both inputs vary by about 2.5 points between seeds. TF-IDF cross-validation agrees (76.24% for output only, 76.74% for both). The output text carries the signal, and the prompt adds nothing here.

## RQ3. Do LLM fingerprints generalize across tasks or domains? (extra credit)

Only partly, and not at all from text to code. I ran three tests. First, models trained on all text domains were tested on 400 held-out code ids. Second, a leave-one-domain-out run trained on seven text domains, selected on their validation ids, and scored on all 2,000 rows of the eighth. Third, the grouped example from the brief trained on news, wiki and reviews and tested on poetry. Hyperparameters were reused from the all-domains tuning, which had already seen every domain's validation ids, so the held-out domains were seen indirectly. Each fold is a single split.

In the leave-one-domain-out runs, mean held-out accuracy was 42.9% for TF-IDF at the 256-token cap, 45.7% for TF-IDF without truncation, 39.9% for the CNN and 39.3% for the BiLSTM. The in-domain controls (the all-domains model on the same domain's original test ids) were 73% to 79%, so every model loses about 33 to 35 points on average. Some signal survives in most domains, with reviews at 58% to 63%, news at 45% to 56% and books at 46% to 54%. Recipes is the exception. The CNN and BiLSTM sit at chance (20.0% and 18.9%) and send 98% to 99% of rows to one class, and TF-IDF reaches only 24% to 27%. A high share of predictions in one class means the model collapsed and did not transfer. The neural models do not generalize better than the linear baseline, and they collapse more often (a mean top-prediction share of 58% to 59%, against 49% to 50% for TF-IDF). Among the LLMs, cohere-chat is the easiest to recognize in a new domain (mean recall 77% to 87%), while llama-chat, mistral-chat and mpt-chat average 19% to 43%, 22% to 29% and 20% to 27%. Per-class recall ranges from 0% to 100% across folds, so one class often absorbs a whole domain.

The grouped example gives 20.8% to 23.8% for all four models, with 91% to 98% of rows in one class. The seven-domain fold that holds out poetry scores higher, at 25.8% to 38.6%, but that gap cannot be credited to domain diversity, because the seven-domain model also trained on much more data.

The text-to-code shift is the extreme case. TF-IDF scores 19.55%, the CNN 20.02% and the BiLSTM 20.02%, against 66% to 76% on in-domain text. For every true class, 94.5% to 98% of predictions are cohere-chat, and a length-and-format-only model collapses the same way (93.8%). Code outputs are much shorter (28 to 37 words against 162 to 279 for text), rarely reach the token cap, and have 13.9% out-of-vocabulary tokens against 2.9% for held-out text. Code is attributable in principle, since TF-IDF trained and cross-validated on code alone reaches 49.4% (plus or minus 2.3) with only 320 training ids per fold. The learned signal therefore looks task-specific and not a general fingerprint, at least for this shift. Code is one domain of short snippets, so the length and format shift is confounded with the domain shift, and I did not separate them.

## RQ4. What characteristics distinguish the LLMs? (extra credit)

Both structure and word choice distinguish them, and word choice carries most of the signal. Length alone gives 37.7% with logistic regression and 51.9% with gradient boosting. Adding 22 stylometric features (length, newlines, bullets, markdown, type-token ratio, punctuation and similar) raises gradient boosting to 65.3%, and TF-IDF word 1 to 2-grams reach 75.8%. To check how much of this is length, I scored a test subset of 820 rows where every class has the same length distribution. There, length-only boosting falls from 51.9% to 39.4% and the stylometric model from 65.3% to 60.2%, while TF-IDF drops only from 75.8% to 74.4% and the CNN from 71.8% to 70.4%. Length matters a great deal for the shallow models and little for TF-IDF. Length does differ strongly between models: mpt-chat averages 162 words and llama-chat 279, and 87% of llama-chat outputs hit the 256-token cap against 27% for mpt-chat. The most informative n-grams mix boilerplate (cohere's "Here is", llama's "Sure" and "Here's") with ordinary content words, and only about 1 to 9 of each class's top 30 features are formatting or boilerplate. Lexical diversity, measured with length-robust statistics (moving-average type-token ratio over 50-word windows, plus type-token ratio and hapax share over the first 100 words), reaches 30.3% alone and moves the stylometric model from 65.25% to 65.83%, within the confidence intervals. It is a weak and mostly redundant cue.

Removing surface cues barely changes TF-IDF, while removing text does. Lowercasing (75.62%), stripping markdown, bullets and newline tokens (76.21%), and masking phrases such as "Cohere", "as an AI" and "Sure! Here" (75.96%) all stay within noise of the 75.75% base (Holm p = 1). Keeping only the first 32, 64 or 128 tokens lowers accuracy to 55.5%, 59.7% and 69.8%, and keeping all tokens raises it to 78.7%. For the neural models, lowercasing costs the CNN 0.08 points and the BiLSTM 2.46 points (p = 0.018, the one significant surface effect), and retraining the CNN on format-normalized input costs 1.38 points, which is not significant. The largest effect in the study is therefore the amount of output text a model sees. These ablations show what a classifier can do without a cue, not which cue a given prediction relied on.

Two further tests intervene on the models directly. In a gradient boosting model with 22 stylometric and 3 diversity features (65.83% with all 25), removing the layout group (newlines, bullets, markdown and line counts) costs the most, down to 61.0%. Removing punctuation costs 2.5 points, lexical features 1.9, opening and ending features 1.8, and length only 0.5. Layout alone scores 52.5%, and on the length-matched subset it keeps 43.7% while length alone falls to 25.2%, so layout is the structural cue that survives length control. Permutation importance ranks length (20.2 points) and layout (19.8 points) first, but the two overlap, so the removal results are the safer reading.

The second test deletes tokens from the inputs of the trained CNN (3-seed ensemble, 72.54% intact). Deleting newline tokens changes 79% of rows and lowers accuracy by 12.3 points to 60.25%, against 2.75 points for the last 32 tokens and 5.58 for 32 random tokens. The drop is concentrated in llama-chat (true-class probability down 43 points) and mistral-chat (down 15), while chatgpt and mpt-chat gain slightly. Deleting markdown markers or boilerplate openers costs under 0.5 points. Deleting the first 32 tokens costs 5.42 points, about the same as 32 random tokens, so the opening is not special beyond ordinary text loss. This looks at odds with the small cost of format normalization above, but the two tests ask different questions. Deleting newlines at inference pushes inputs away from what the CNN was trained on, so the drop shows that the trained model uses line structure, mainly to recognize llama-chat. A model retrained without that structure recovers almost all of its accuracy, so the cue is learned but largely redundant with other signals in the text.

These findings describe what this CNN relies on (one architecture, three seeds, a 256-token cap) and not what separates the LLMs in general. Token deletion shifts the remaining positions and produces unnatural text, and the feature-group results come from a single boosting fit per feature set.
